fund-csharp/21-testing-web-apps-seams-an.explanation:
The clock is just another dependency. The fake keeps one instant and moves it only when told:

```csharp
public override DateTimeOffset GetUtcNow() => now;
public void Advance(TimeSpan by)
{
    if (by < TimeSpan.Zero) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(by));
    now += by;
}
```

The service reads it once and compares with `<`:

```csharp
var now = clock.GetUtcNow();
return new Invite(email, now, now.AddDays(7));
// Accept:
clock.GetUtcNow() < invite.ExpiresAt ? 200 : 410;
```

The tests catch three plausible wrong versions: two reads of the clock (the ticking clock makes `ExpiresAt - CreatedAt` one millisecond too long), `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` anywhere (the 2001 clock is ignored), and `<=` in `Accept` (the invite is still accepted at the exact expiry instant).

